            "Malaria is caused by protozoa of the genus <em>Plasmodium</em> transmitted by the bite of an infected female <strong><em>Anopheles</em></strong> mosquito — in Ghana chiefly <em>An. gambiae</em> and <em>An. funestus</em>, which bite between dusk and dawn, mostly indoors, and breed in clean, still, sunlit water: puddles, hoof-prints, rice fields, construction pits and blocked gutters after the rains. More than nine in ten Ghanaian infections are <strong><em>P. falciparum</em></strong>, the species that kills; <em>P. malariae</em> and <em>P. ovale</em> cause a minority, and <em>P. vivax</em> is rare because most West Africans lack the Duffy antigen it needs to enter red cells. Transmission is <strong>perennial</strong> in the forest and coastal south, with peaks after the rains, and <strong>seasonal</strong> in the savannah north, where it is concentrated in the four or five months of the rainy season — the pattern that makes seasonal chemoprevention possible there. The whole population is at risk; the deaths are concentrated in children under five, pregnant women, people with sickle cell disease and HIV, and visitors from non-endemic areas who have no acquired immunity.",
